Now, eight years later, this Leiphone longform essay (link to *original
Chinese
<https://substack.com/redirect/efe225c1-88d2-4b53-a94d-f6c0523b5fc3?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*)
demonstrates that Chinese companies are still chasing Palantir =E2=80=94 wh=
ich has
seen a stock increase of 15-fold since its IPO =E2=80=94and the gap is stil=
l very
very wide.

*KEY TAKEAWAYS: Why do so many Chinese AI firms see Palantir as a
standard-bearer?*

   -

   They want to emulate Palantir=E2=80=99s path from a government-facing bu=
siness
   foundation (basically all of its initial clients were defense,
   intelligence, and law enforcement agencies) to a company that now derive=
s
   46% of its revenues from commercial businesses (To-B).
   -

   A key component of the Palantir model is the forward deployed engineer
   (FDE). From the article: =E2=80=9C*The FDE model also originated from Pa=
lantir=E2=80=99s
   early To-G operations: in military and government contexts=E2=80=94chara=
cterized by
   data silos and complex operational environments=E2=80=94conventional sof=
tware
   development models proved ineffective. Consequently, Palantir was compel=
led
   to dispatch personnel to the front lines to gain a firsthand understandi=
ng
   of operational requirements, subsequently bringing those insights back t=
o
   be distilled and abstracted into standardized products*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   The Palantir =E2=80=9Ccraze has also rapidly swept through the domestic =
Chinese
   market, =E2=80=9C Jiamin Zhang, the Leiphone article author, reports. =
=E2=80=9CVendors such
   as 4Paradigm, MiningLamp, and Deepexi [=E6=BB=B4=E6=99=AE=E7=A7=91=E6=8A=
=80] have rushed to benchmark
   against the company=E2=80=A6=E2=80=99

*According to industry insiders, there are three groups of competitors
seeking to become China=E2=80=99s Palantir:*

   1.

   The companies who mirror Palantir=E2=80=99s business approach (e.g., the
   aforementioned MiningLamp, 4Paradigm, etc.).
   2.

   Companies that focus on the defense + AI vertical, including Jing'an
   Technology [=E9=9D=96=E5=AE=89=E7=A7=91=E6=8A=80], Utenet [=E6=B8=8A=E4=
=BA=AD=E7=A7=91=E6=8A=80], and Stonehenge [=E4=B8=AD=E7=A7=91=E4=B8=96=E9=
=80=9A=E4=BA=A8=E5=A5=87].
   3.

   Large tech giants such as Huawei and Baidu who may be the only
   candidates with resources to do what Palantir does =E2=80=94 a =E2=80=9C=
heavyweight=E2=80=9D
   approach with those FDEs. According to Leiphone, =E2=80=9C*Reportedly, a=
 team
   within a certain major Chinese tech giant is already actively benchmarki=
ng
   against Palantir to develop relevant products*.=E2=80=9D

*Yet, these Palantir wannabes face many obstacles. There are many reasons
why =E2=80=9CChina has yet to produce a software giant with global influenc=
e
comparable to Palantir or Salesforce.=E2=80=9D *Chinese firms fight for scr=
aps in
China=E2=80=99s low-end software market, while global giants dominate the h=
igh-end.

   -

   AI doesn=E2=80=99t just magically solve diffusion challenges rooted in C=
hina=E2=80=99s
   lower rates of digitization. Menglin Li, VP at Mininglamp, tells Leiphon=
e
   that Western firms have more eagerly embraced =E2=80=9Ccloud-nativeness=
=E2=80=9D, which
   means their data infrastructure are more uniform and standardized than
   Chinese companies. The article continues, =E2=80=9C*Li reveals that on t=
he very
   first day of providing on-site services to a domestic client, Mininglamp=
's
   team must first undertake the painstaking task of deciphering exactly ho=
w
   the client's legacy systems were originally constructed and what underly=
ing
   technical architectures were employed*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Zhang Chi, an industry expert with over 20 years of experience, points
   out that the Palantir model does not travel to China. Specifically, Chin=
ese
   companies aren=E2=80=99t willing to pay the large-scale consulting contr=
acts that
   involve deploying industry experts to client sites and then
updating s=C2=B9oftware
   products based on feedback. Zhang states, =E2=80=9C*No enterprise here w=
ould
   issue a $1 billion contract to a vendor, especially if the work consiste=
d
   primarily of consulting, on-site deployment, and project delivery*.=E2=
=80=9D
   -

   The FDE approach is also difficult to implement in terms of
   civil-military integration. I think people just assume that because Chin=
a
   has a Military-Civil Fusion strategy, it means there=E2=80=99s seamless
   integrations between the Mininglamps of the world and the PLA. That=E2=
=80=99s just
   not the case. Menglin Li, the previously quoted Mininglamp VP,
states, =E2=80=9C*Palantir
   is willing to embed its own employees directly into the U.S. military,
   where they serve as commissioned officers=E2=80=94a concept that is almo=
st
   unimaginable in China*.=E2=80=9D

